<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valladolid, Yucatán</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valladolid is a city in the east of the Mexican state of Yucatán, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-nephew">Francisco de Montejo the Nephew</a> in 1543 at Chauaca and moved in 1545 to the Maya town of Saci. Built over Saci's temples, it became the Spanish centre of eastern Yucatán. In 1847 it was the scene of the violence that opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste-war-of-yucatan">Caste War of Yucatán</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valladolid-yucatan.jpg" alt="Valladolid, Yucatán" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Addicted04 (collage) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valladolid_Yucatan_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> lies in the eastern region of the Mexican state of Yucatán and is the seat of Valladolid Municipality. It stands about 170 km east of the state capital Mérida, 40 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichén Itzá</a> and 150 km west of Cancún. Its Maya name is Sakiʼ. It was named after Valladolid, then the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It lies inland, in a hot climate with a rainy season from June to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spanish city was established on 27 May 1543 by the nephew of the conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a>, at some distance from its present site, beside a lagoon in the municipality of Tizimín. The early settlers complained of mosquitoes and humidity and asked for the city to be moved inland. On 24 March 1545 it was relocated to its present site, on a Maya town whose buildings were dismantled so that the stones could be reused for the colonial town. The Maya rose the following year, but the revolt was put down with extra Spanish troops from Mérida.</p><p>In 1705 local Maya rebelled again and killed a number of town officials who had taken refuge in the cathedral. After the revolt was suppressed the cathedral was judged irreparably profaned and was demolished. A new one was built the next year, and it still stands. It faces north, unlike most colonial churches in Yucatán, which face east.</p><p>The city had 15,000 inhabitants in 1840. In January 1847 Maya rioted and killed some eighty whites; after a Maya noble was shot by firing squad the riot turned into a general uprising led by Jacinto Pat, batab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihosuco">Tihosuco</a>, and Cecilio Chi of Ichmul. Valladolid was the scene of intense fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste-war-of-yucatan">Caste War of Yucatán</a>. Ladino forces were forced to abandon it on 14 March 1848, and half were killed in an ambush before reaching Mérida. Maya rebels sacked it, and it was recaptured later in the war. Until the early 20th century it was the third-largest city of the peninsula, after Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a>, with a prosperous Criollo population, and was nicknamed the Sultana of the East.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sights include the Convent of San Bernardino de Siena, built by Franciscan missionaries between 1552 and 1560, which is linked to the centre by the 16th-century Calzada de los Frailes, and the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Saint Servatius</a> on the main square. The Cenote Zací, a freshwater sinkhole, lies close to the centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> belongs to the federal Pueblos Mágicos programme and is a base for visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichén Itzá</a>, Ekʼ Balam and Cenote Ik Kil. Its regional dishes include cochinita pibil, lomitos de Valladolid and longaniza sausage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Valladolid, Yucatán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Francisco de Montejo the Nephew conquered Chikinchel in 1543-44 and founded the first <b>Valladolid</b> at Chauaca, moving it to Saci in 1545.</blockquote><a href="/state/chikinchel">Chikinchel</a></li><li><blockquote>It covered a large area of east-central Yucatán, including the ruins of Chichen Itza, with its main town at Saci, where <b>Valladolid</b> now stands.</blockquote><a href="/state/cupul">Cupul</a></li></ul></section>
